  "//overrides": "wdk-react-native-core@beta.15 targets a newer toolchain than this starter (Expo SDK 54, react-native-nitro-modules 0.32.1), so three of its transitive native modules have to be held back. (1) react-native-mmkv: from 4.1.2 onward its generated Swift references nitro's RecyclableView, which only exists in nitro >= 0.33.0, so the iOS build fails to compile NitroMmkvAutolinking.swift; 4.1.0 predates that symbol and still exposes everything core uses (createMMKV({ id, encryptionKey }), set, getString, remove, contains, getAllKeys, clearAll). (2+3) expo-crypto and expo-local-authentication: Expo switched to SDK-matched major versions at SDK 55, so core's expo-crypto@^56 and expo-local-authentication@^55 are SDK 56/55 packages. Autolinking silently skips them on SDK 54 — no pod is generated and the app dies at runtime with \"Cannot find native module 'ExpoCrypto'\". The pinned versions are the SDK 54 equivalents. Drop all three once the starter moves to nitro >= 0.33 and Expo SDK 56.",
  "//overrides-orchestra": "wdk-protocol-swidge-orchestra pins @tetherto/wdk-wallet@1.0.0-beta.11, so npm nests a second copy under it and the worklet bundle ends up with two SwidgeProtocol classes. WDK's registerProtocol() gates on `Protocol.prototype instanceof SwidgeProtocol` and silently registers nothing when the identities differ — every RPC reports success but getSwidgeProtocol('orchestra') throws 'No swidge protocol registered'. Forcing the hoisted beta.15 (base class diff is additive-only: optional minAmountOut) restores a single class identity.",
  "overrides": {
    "react-native-mmkv": "4.1.0",
    "expo-crypto": "~15.0.9",
    "expo-local-authentication": "~17.0.8",
    "wdk-protocol-swidge-orchestra": {
      "@tetherto/wdk-wallet": "1.0.0-beta.15"
    },
    "@tetherto/wdk-wallet-solana": {
      "@tetherto/wdk-wallet": "1.0.0-beta.15"
    },
    "react-server-dom-webpack": "19.0.9"
  },
  "peerDependencies": {
    "react": "19.1.0",
    "react-native": "0.81.4"
  }
